{
  "dom_id": "step-q17961",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/72456f82a1fdf160033fbdfa8c554bd2d9ccc8c8b8350266cb970b829c360e26.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(x)+2?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/1c289a9bc1b7615682dd0667ae0cea8a156b0be1478bdb92690d1987da6c0927.png)\n\n- Then, translate the result up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/e33cff834a6bb28853dfe5454ed7790f8edb501ad3c364ae52ba4a1880566b59.png)",
